module std.durable_compare_and_set

// A durable compare-and-set over one keyed slot: the conditional-write
// primitive, modeled as a shape rather than a transport. The realization may
// be an HTTP conditional request, a filesystem rename, or a row update with a
// version predicate; none of those is a fact about the operation (DESIGN 3 —
// transport is a Realization handler bound to the shape, never fused into it).
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT std.temporal_effect LeaseEpoch, which already carries a
// generation: that generation is a FENCING TOKEN, and a fencing token excludes
// nothing on its own. Holding generation N does not stop another writer from
// acting; it only lets a store REJECT a stale writer, and only if the store
// performs the check. In gunbc.fleet_converge_plan_cli the exclusion that
// actually holds is flock, which is single-host by construction and therefore
// cannot serialize two writers on different hosts. This module is the missing
// half: the store-side check itself, whose refusal arm belongs to the store
// rather than to a lock the caller happens to hold.



type CasGeneration =
  Int where range(min: 1), brand("CasGeneration")

// RUNG, DECLARED: the range(min: 1) above is NOT enforced at compile time —
// the compiler reports it as `where-refinement unenforced: predicate deferred`,
// the pre-existing refinement gap this module does not close. A generation of
// zero or below is therefore writable here, and this carrier sits at
// MITIGATABLE for that class. Next-rung trigger: refinement enforcement on
// branded Int carriers. What is NOT deferred, and is the structural claim this
// module actually makes, is the absent-versus-present distinction below: that
// one has no representation to get wrong, refinement or no refinement.

// The expected prior state, as a closed sum rather than a number.
//
// This is the structural half of the module. A store that encodes "absent" as
// generation 0 has made absence and a real generation members of one ordered
// type, so a caller that means create-if-absent and a caller that means
// update-from-0 are indistinguishable — and one of them silently overwrites
// the other's row. Here ExpectSlotAbsent is a distinct constructor, so that
// conflation has no representation to be written in. Create-if-absent and
// update-if-unchanged are then ONE operation with two expectations, not two
// operations (DESIGN 2, horizontal).
type CasExpectation
  = ExpectSlotAbsent
  | ExpectSlotGeneration { generation: CasGeneration }

// The readable slots, and the only thing the decision helpers below accept.
//
// This exists because the helpers used to take the full CasSlotObservation and
// answer for its unreadable arms — cas_committed_generation returned
// generation 1 for a slot it could not read, which is precisely the number a
// writer with no knowledge of the current head would use to clobber it. That
// the intended path never reached those arms (cas_decide classifies first)
// made the refusal a CALLER-ORDERING property rather than a construction one:
// any later realization importing the helper directly would recreate the
// narrow this module exists to forbid. Validation standing where construction
// was available, in the module whose whole job is to make the class unwritable.
//
// Narrowing the input deletes the arms instead of checking them. The
// classification cas_decide already performs is now a type transition, so the
// ordering that used to be a convention is the only way to obtain the
// argument at all.
type CasReadableSlot<T>
  = CasReadableAbsent
  | CasReadablePresent { version: CasSlotVersion<T> }

// One slot's committed content. It deliberately does NOT carry the slot key.
//
// It used to. That made the key a fact stored twice — once on the attempt and
// once on the observed version — and two copies of one fact can disagree,
// which was a live wrong-slot commit path because cas_decide compared
// generations only. Deleting the duplicate was right and DESIGN 3 is the
// reason.
//
// CORRECTION, and it matters because the earlier wording claimed more than the
// code delivers: deleting the key did NOT make a wrong-slot commit
// unconstructible. An unkeyed observation describes NO slot, which is not the
// same as describing THE slot the attempt names. cas_decide_readable never
// reads attempt.key, so an observation actually read from slot B still commits
// to slot A when the generations agree. What the deletion removed is the
// EVIDENCE of that mismatch, not its possibility — and it is weaker in one
// respect than what preceded it, because a refusal arm could have caught the
// disagreement while two copies existed and nothing can catch it now.
//
// The relation between an observation and the slot it was read from is
// therefore OUTSIDE THE MODELED GUARANTEE at this layer, exactly like the
// content/digest pairing below. See key_relation_boundary_note.
type CasSlotVersion<T> {
  generation: CasGeneration
  content: std.content_hash.ContentHash
  value: T
}

// What the store found, as readable-or-not rather than five flat arms.
//
// The two carriers below are the authority for each half, and this type is
// their sum — NOT a third enumeration restating them. Listing the five arms
// here as well would put the same information in two representations, which
// is the duplication DESIGN 3 forbids; adding CasUnreadableSlot beside a flat
// five-arm observation would have created exactly that.
//
// "The slot holds nothing" and "I could not determine what the slot holds" are
// different states with opposite remedies, and rendering the second as the
// first is the empty-observation narrow (DESIGN 5) — a writer would create a
// row on top of one it merely failed to read.
type CasSlotObservation<T>
  = CasObservedReadable { readable: CasReadableSlot<T> }
  | CasObservedUnreadable { cause: CasUnreadableSlot }

// Why the store could not read the slot, kept typed all the way out to the
// caller.
//
// These are not one arm with a `detail` string: a malformed head is repaired
// by hand, a missing referenced object is a write that did not land and may be
// re-derived, and a refused read is retried against the store. Collapsing them
// into prose would force a caller to parse a String to choose a remedy — the
// anemic leaf DESIGN 2 names. This carrier exists because cas_decide used to
// do precisely that: it mapped all three onto CasStoreRefused { detail }, so
// the module erased one layer out the distinction it made one layer in.
type CasUnreadableSlot
  = CasUnreadableMalformed { detail: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | CasUnreadableContentMissing { referenced: std.content_hash.ContentHash }
  | CasUnreadableReadRefused { detail: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

// The outcome of one attempt.
//
// There is deliberately no arm meaning "retried and assume it worked" and no
// arm meaning "proceed as if the precondition had held". Every non-committed
// arm is a typed, located refusal the caller must handle; a widening arm here
// would be the absorbing fallback (DESIGN 5) sitting on the exact operation
// whose whole purpose is to refuse.
//
// CasPreconditionFailed carries BOTH sides — what was expected and what was
// observed — because a caller that only learns "it failed" cannot distinguish
// losing a race from addressing the wrong slot, and would retry forever
// against the second.
type CasOutcome<T>
  = CasCommitted { committed: CasSlotVersion<T> }
  | CasPreconditionFailed {
      expected: CasExpectation
      observed: CasReadableSlot<T>
    }
  | CasStoreRefused { cause: CasUnreadableSlot }

type CasAttempt<T> {
  key: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  expected: CasExpectation
  proposed: T
  proposed_content: std.content_hash.ContentHash
}

fn cas_attempt<T>(
  key: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  expected: CasExpectation,
  proposed: T,
  proposed_content: std.content_hash.ContentHash
) -> CasAttempt<T> {
  CasAttempt {
    key: key,
    expected: expected,
    proposed: proposed,
    proposed_content: proposed_content
  }
}

// The decision an in-memory store realization makes, and the single authority
// on whether an expectation is satisfied by an observation. A realization that
// re-spelled this comparison would be a second representation of the
// precondition (DESIGN 3); every handler routes here.
//
// An unreadable slot cannot reach this function at all — it is not in
// CasReadableSlot. That is the point: this used to accept the full
// observation and answer `false` for the unreadable arms, which reported an
// absence of evidence as a lost race. The arms are now gone rather than
// handled, and cas_decide routes unreadability to CasStoreRefused before any
// classification happens.
fn cas_expectation_admits<T>(
  expected: CasExpectation,
  readable: CasReadableSlot<T>
) -> Bool {
  match readable {
    CasReadableAbsent => match expected {
      ExpectSlotAbsent => true
      ExpectSlotGeneration { generation: _ } => false
    }
    CasReadablePresent { version: v } => match expected {
      ExpectSlotAbsent => false
      ExpectSlotGeneration { generation: g } => cas_generation_count(g: g) == cas_generation_count(g: v.generation)
    }
  }
}

fn cas_generation_count(g: CasGeneration) -> Int {
  g
}

fn cas_generation_next(g: CasGeneration) -> CasGeneration {
  cas_generation_count(g: g) + 1
}

fn cas_generation_first() -> CasGeneration {
  1
}

// The generation a successful commit lands on, derived from the observation
// rather than from the caller's expectation — a caller cannot name the
// generation it is about to occupy.
fn cas_committed_generation<T>(readable: CasReadableSlot<T>) -> CasGeneration {
  match readable {
    CasReadableAbsent => cas_generation_first()
    CasReadablePresent { version: v } => cas_generation_next(g: v.generation)
  }
}

fn cas_decide<T>(
  attempt: CasAttempt<T>,
  observed: CasSlotObservation<T>
) -> CasOutcome<T> {
  match observed {
    CasObservedUnreadable { cause: c } => CasStoreRefused { cause: c }
    CasObservedReadable { readable: r } => cas_decide_readable(attempt: attempt, readable: r)
  }
}

// BOUNDARY OBLIGATION, DECLARED — the observation-to-slot relation.
//
// CasAttempt carries `key`, and no decision in this module reads it. The fold
// cannot: an unkeyed CasReadableSlot carries nothing to compare the key
// against, so the relation "this observation was read from the slot this
// attempt names" is unrepresentable here rather than merely unchecked.
//
// The existence of CasAttempt.key is itself the proof that the guarantee is
// not free. "Only one slot can be described" would hold only for a store with
// exactly one global slot — and such a store would not need the field.
//
// Sealing it requires the store, which owns the read: either a store-minted
// bound observation whose key relation a caller cannot forge, or folding read
// and conditional-write into ONE store-owned operation so no caller ever
// supplies an observation separately. The second is the shape the realization
// should take: compare_and_set(attempt) -> CasOutcome, with the store reading
// attempt.key itself.
//
// Candidate wall: `sole_constructor`, so only the store can mint the bound
// observation. Until then this is observed and refused at the store boundary,
// never proven here — and the witness
// `known_gap_attempt_key_unconsumed_red_here_means_the_gap_was_closed` keeps the gap executable
// rather than leaving it as prose. That control dissolves into the
// construction evidence when a bound observation makes the pairing unformable.

// BOUNDARY OBLIGATION, DECLARED — not a rung, and not fixable here.
//
// Nothing in this module proves that `proposed_content` is the hash OF
// `proposed`, so a caller can pair state A with the digest of state B and the
// fold will commit it. The same gap exists between CasSlotVersion's `content`
// and `value`.
//
// It cannot be closed by construction in a pure generic fold: std.content_hash
// exposes `content_hash_of_value(value: NonEmptyStr) -> ContentHash`, which is
// not generic over T, so no total hashing function over an arbitrary payload
// exists to derive the digest from the value. Deriving it is therefore
// unavailable rather than merely unwritten, and inventing a check here would
// be validation that cannot actually verify anything.
//
// The obligation belongs to the immutable object store that realizes this
// interface: it holds the bytes, so it can verify bytes against digest and
// hand back a reference that only verification produces. The CAS head then
// points at a verified reference rather than a caller-authored pair.
//
// Candidate wall for making the unverified pair unwritable once that store
// exists: `sole_constructor`, the cross-module construction refusal DESIGN 4b
// names as the live unforgeable-construction candidate. Until then this sits
// OUTSIDE the modeled guarantee — observed and refused at the store boundary,
// never proven here.
fn cas_decide_readable<T>(
  attempt: CasAttempt<T>,
  readable: CasReadableSlot<T>
) -> CasOutcome<T> {
  if cas_expectation_admits(expected: attempt.expected, readable: readable) {
    CasCommitted {
      committed: CasSlotVersion {
        generation: cas_committed_generation(readable: readable),
        content: attempt.proposed_content,
        value: attempt.proposed
      }
    }
  } else {
    CasPreconditionFailed { expected: attempt.expected, observed: readable }
  }
}
